I fixed item 2 of #1094: a read_file page that output truncation cut in the middle no longer counts as having been read when the model then tries write_file on the whole file. Build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and fmt all pass. The code is in commit 813a7289, a docs commit 2c22090c follows, and I commented on #1094.

The bug was real. I wrote the test before touching anything. A file of 200 lines × 300 chars, read through the same wrapper stack the main agent uses (with_truncation(maybe_guard(ReadFileTool))), reached the model with its middle cut out (row 120 missing). The guard still let a whole-file write through, because it records coverage from yoagent's uncut header before truncation runs.

The fix:
• Where the check runs: for read_file only, TruncatingTool keeps the result from before truncation and calls a new partial_read_guard::retract_cut. If the lines the model receives differ from what read_file returned, the page it just recorded is taken back out.
• What it catches: it compares the text itself, not which cut happened, so the head/tail cut, the hard byte cut and compression all count.
• What I didn't do: I didn't record the kept head and tail as partial coverage. It would take more lines, and there's no line span to work out when the change came from compression.
• Earlier reads stay: ranges are now stored unmerged, so only that one page is removed and anything read before it still counts.
• The model can recover: the refusal now says a truncated page doesn't count and to re-read with a smaller limit, so it can't get stuck on a refusal it has no way to satisfy.
• I didn't reorder any wrappers in tools.rs. Sub-agents' read_file has no truncation, so it behaves as before.

Tests: I added five rows to src/partial_read_guard_tests.rs:
• a cut page is refused;
• a page under the 30k-char limit is admitted exactly as before;
• a cut page followed by 50-line pages is admitted;
• the refusal names the path, offset/limit and a smaller limit;
• a cut takes back only its own page.

As a check that the tests can fail, I disabled retract_cut and re-ran them in one step: exactly the three cut-page tests went red, and after restoring it all 22 passed with no NEUTERED marker left.

Other files changed:
• Existing test: one test had the full refusal text written out exactly, so I updated it to the new text.
• Size register: tests/module_size.rs records src/tool_wrappers.rs at 5713 → 5724 lines (+11).
• Docs: ARCHITECTURE.md marks item 2 fixed and keeps the old "open" claim on record as superseded. docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md says a truncated page doesn't count as read.

The docs commit went in after the full test run, and I checked that no test reads safety.md.

Items 1, 3 and 4 of #1094 are still open: an existing file that was never read can still be overwritten, the store is shared across the whole process (a sub-agent's reads count for the parent), and a change made by bash is only checked by line count.
